  <worksheet xml:id="activity-01-intro-activity">
  <title> Exercises for <xref ref="section-taylor" text="title"/></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      If you would like to review this topic before attempting the exercises,
      click <xref ref="section-taylor" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- Exercises start here.  --> 

  <!-- Review problems on Taylor polynomials and their error. -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-6">
<statement>
<p>
For approximately what values of <m>x</m> can we replace
<m>\sin(x)</m> by <m>x - \dfrac{x^3}{6}</m> with an error of magnitude
no greater than <m>4 \times 10^{-3}</m>?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here <m>a = 0</m>, since <m>T_3(x) = x - \dfrac{x^3}{3!}</m>. The
derivatives of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> are
<md>
<mrow>f'(x) \amp = \cos x, \amp
f''(x) \amp = -\sin(x),</mrow>
<mrow>f^{(3)}(x) \amp = -\cos(x), \amp
f^{(4)}(x) \amp = \sin(x),</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<m>\left|f^{(4)}(c)\right| = |\sin(c)| \le 1</m>, so we can take
<m>M = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
By the Remainder Theorem,
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_3(x)| \le M\,\frac{|x-a|^4}{4!}
= \frac{|x|^4}{4!} \le 4\times 10^{-3},
</md>
which gives
<md>
|x| \le \sqrt[4]{(4!)(0.004)} \approx 0.556
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
x \in [-0.556,\; 0.556].
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-rev-sin-approx"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-sin-approx">
<caption>
The function <m>y = \sin x</m> and the cubic <m>y = x - \frac{x^3}{6}</m>.
Between the dashed lines <m>x = \pm 0.556</m> the two graphs differ by
at most <m>4\times 10^{-3}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="78%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-sin-approx">
<diagram dimensions="(400,270)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, sin(t))</definition>
<definition>p(t) = (t, t - t^3/6)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.7,-1.7,2.7,1.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="sa-left" endpoints="((-0.556,-1.45),(-0.556,1.45))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="sa-right" endpoints="((0.556,-1.45),(0.556,1.45))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="sa-p" function="p" domain="(-2.6,2.6)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="sa-f" function="f" domain="(-2.6,2.6)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
<label at="sa-f-lab" anchor="(-2.6,1.65)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=\sin x</m>
</label>
<label at="sa-p-lab" anchor="(-2.6,1.25)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=x-\frac{x^3}{6}</m>
</label>
<label at="sa-l-lab" anchor="(-0.62,-1.62)" alignment="w">
<m>-0.556</m>
</label>
<label at="sa-r-lab" anchor="(0.62,-1.62)" alignment="e">
<m>0.556</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The sine curve, dashed blue, and the cubic x minus x cubed over six, solid vermillion, plotted together. Two dashed gray vertical lines at x equals plus and minus zero point five five six mark the interval on which the two curves are visually indistinguishable; outside it the cubic pulls away from the sine curve.">
  <annotation ref="sa-f" text="The function y equals sine x."/>
  <annotation ref="sa-p" text="The cubic Taylor polynomial x minus x cubed over six."/>
  <annotation ref="sa-left" text="The left endpoint of the interval, x equals negative zero point five five six."/>
  <annotation ref="sa-right" text="The right endpoint of the interval, x equals zero point five five six. Inside the interval the error is at most four thousandths."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-7">
<statement>
<p>
Use the remainder estimation theorem to estimate the maximum error when
approximating <m>f(x) = e^x</m> by <m>T_2(x) = 1 + x + x^2</m> on the
interval <m>\left[-\dfrac56, \dfrac56\right]</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
From the remainder theorem, we know that
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
= \left|\frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!}\,x^3\right|
\le \frac{e^c}{3!}\,|x|^3.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Also, since <m>c \in \left[-\frac56, \frac56\right]</m>, we have
<m>|c| \le \frac56</m> and hence <m>e^c \le e^{5/6}</m>:
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
\le \frac{e^{5/6}}{3!}\left(\frac56\right)^{3}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
We know that <m>e^{5/6} \lt e^1 \lt 3</m>, hence
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
\le \frac{e^{5/6}}{3!}\left(\frac56\right)^{3}
\lt \frac{3}{3!}\left(\frac56\right)^{3} \approx 0.289.
</md>
Hence, an estimate for the upper bound of the error is
<m>\dfrac{3}{3!}\left(\dfrac56\right)^{3} \approx 0.289</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-exp-approx"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-exp-approx">
<caption>
The function <m>y = e^x</m> and <m>y = T_2(x) = 1 + x + x^2</m> on the
interval <m>\left[-\frac56, \frac56\right]</m>, marked by the dashed
lines.
</caption>
<image width="76%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-exp-approx">
<diagram dimensions="(390,270)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, exp(t))</definition>
<definition>p(t) = (t, 1 + t + t^2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.6,-0.5,1.6,3.4)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="ea-left" endpoints="((-0.8333,-0.25),(-0.8333,3.1))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ea-right" endpoints="((0.8333,-0.25),(0.8333,3.1))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ea-p" function="p" domain="(-1.5,1.25)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ea-f" function="f" domain="(-1.5,1.2)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
<label at="ea-f-lab" anchor="(-1.5,3.2)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=e^{x}</m>
</label>
<label at="ea-p-lab" anchor="(-1.5,2.75)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=1+x+x^2</m>
</label>
<label at="ea-l-lab" anchor="(-0.88,-0.42)" alignment="w">
<m>-\frac56</m>
</label>
<label at="ea-r-lab" anchor="(0.88,-0.42)" alignment="e">
<m>\frac56</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The exponential curve, dashed blue, and the quadratic one plus x plus x squared, solid vermillion, plotted together. Dashed gray vertical lines at plus and minus five sixths mark the interval of the approximation; on that interval the two curves stay within about zero point three of each other.">
  <annotation ref="ea-f" text="The function y equals e to the x."/>
  <annotation ref="ea-p" text="The quadratic approximation one plus x plus x squared."/>
  <annotation ref="ea-left" text="The left endpoint, x equals negative five sixths."/>
  <annotation ref="ea-right" text="The right endpoint, x equals five sixths. The error on the interval is at most about zero point two eight nine."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-8">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x) = \ln(1 + 2x)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the second order Taylor polynomial <m>T_2(x)</m> centered at 0.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate the maximum error when approximating
<m>f(x) = \ln(1+2x)</m> with the second order Taylor polynomial
<m>T_2(x)</m> centered at 0, on the interval <m>[-0.25, 0.25]</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We compute the derivatives at 0:
<md>
<mrow>f'(x) \amp = \frac{2}{1+2x} = 2(1+2x)^{-1},
\amp f'(0) \amp = 2,</mrow>
<mrow>f''(x) \amp = -4(1+2x)^{-2},
\amp f''(0) \amp = -4.</mrow>
</md>
Hence
<md>
T_2(x) = f(0) + f'(0)(x-0) + \frac{f''(0)}{2!}(x-0)^2
= 0 + 2x - 2x^2,
</md>
i.e. <m>T_2(x) = 2x - 2x^2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We have <m>f^{(3)}(x) = 8(1+2x)^{-3}</m> and
<m>-\frac14 \le x \le \frac14</m>. Note that
<m>f^{(4)}(x) = -24(1+2x)^{-4}</m> is negative, which means
<m>f^{(3)}</m> is a decreasing function, hence at <m>x = -\frac14</m>
the value <m>f^{(3)}(c)</m> is maximized, i.e.,
<md>
f^{(3)}(c) \le f^{(3)}(-0.25)
= 8\left(1 - \frac12\right)^{-3} = 64.
</md>
Also
<md>
-\frac14 \le x \le \frac14
\;\Rightarrow\;
|x^3| \le \left(\frac14\right)^{3} = \frac{1}{4^3} = \frac{1}{64},
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
\le \left|\frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!}\,x^3\right|
\le \frac{1}{3!}\cdot\frac{1}{64}\cdot 64 = \frac16.
</md>
This means that the error in estimating <m>f(x) = \ln(1+2x)</m> for
<m>-\frac14 \le x \le \frac14</m> by <m>T_2(x) = 2x - 2x^2</m> is
definitely smaller than <m>\frac16</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-ln-approx"/>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-ln-approx">
<caption>
The function <m>y = \ln(1+2x)</m> and <m>y = T_2(x) = 2x - 2x^2</m>. On
the interval <m>[-0.25, 0.25]</m>, marked by the dashed lines, the two
graphs are nearly identical.
</caption>
<image width="76%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-ln-approx">
<diagram dimensions="(390,260)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, log(1 + 2*t))</definition>
<definition>p(t) = (t, 2*t - 2*t^2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.52,-1.5,0.75,1.1)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="la-left" endpoints="((-0.25,-1.3),(-0.25,0.9))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="la-right" endpoints="((0.25,-1.3),(0.25,0.9))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="la-p" function="p" domain="(-0.45,0.72)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="la-f" function="f" domain="(-0.44,0.72)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
<label at="la-f-lab" anchor="(0.30,0.98)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=\ln(1+2x)</m>
</label>
<label at="la-p-lab" anchor="(0.30,0.70)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=2x-2x^2</m>
</label>
<label at="la-l-lab" anchor="(-0.27,-1.44)" alignment="w">
<m>-0.25</m>
</label>
<label at="la-r-lab" anchor="(0.27,-1.44)" alignment="e">
<m>0.25</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The curve y equals natural log of one plus two x, dashed blue, and the quadratic two x minus two x squared, solid vermillion. Dashed gray vertical lines at plus and minus one quarter mark the interval of the approximation, where the two curves nearly coincide; outside it they separate, and the logarithm drops steeply near x equals negative one half.">
  <annotation ref="la-f" text="The function y equals natural log of one plus two x."/>
  <annotation ref="la-p" text="The second order Taylor polynomial two x minus two x squared."/>
  <annotation ref="la-left" text="The left endpoint, x equals negative one quarter."/>
  <annotation ref="la-right" text="The right endpoint, x equals one quarter. The error on the interval is smaller than one sixth."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="ex-tf-1">
    <title>True or False</title>
    <statement correct="no">
        <p>If we manage to find the maximum value of <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> for <m>c</m> in the interval between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, 
          then we can find the exact error in the Taylor polynomial approximation.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback>
        <p>The statement is incorrect. While finding the maximum value of <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> allows us to find an upper bound for the error, it does not give the exact error.</p>
    </feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <title>Approximating The Electric Field due to an Electric Dipole</title>
    <statement>
        <p>Consider an electric dipole consisting of two charges, <m>+q</m> and <m>-q</m>, 
          separated by a distance <m>d</m>. The electric field at a point <m>P</m> located at a distance <m>r</m>
          from the positive charge along the axis of the dipole is given by:
<md>
          E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)
        </md>
</p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-dipole-schematic">
  <caption>An electric dipole: charges <m>+q</m> and <m>-q</m> separated by a
  distance <m>d</m>, with the field point <m>P</m> a distance <m>r</m> from
  the positive charge along the axis.</caption>
  <image width="70%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="dipole-schematic">
      <diagram dimensions="(460,200)" margins="8">
        <!-- Dipole schematic: -q at x=-1.1, +q at x=0, point P at x=3.2 on the axis.
             Distances: d (between charges), r (from +q to P). -->
        <definition>xneg = -1.1</definition>
        <definition>xpos = 0</definition>
        <definition>xP = 3.2</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-1.9, -1.0, 4.0, 1.2)" aspect-ratio="1">
          <line at="axis" p1="(-1.7, 0)" p2="(3.8, 0)" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
          <group at="negcharge">
            <point p="(xneg, 0)" fill="blue" size="13"/>
            <label anchor="(xneg, 0)" alignment="c" color="white"><m>-q</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="poscharge">
            <point p="(xpos, 0)" fill="red" size="13"/>
            <label anchor="(xpos, 0)" alignment="c" color="white"><m>+q</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="pointP">
            <point p="(xP, 0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
            <label anchor="(xP, 0)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,4)"><m>P</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="ddist">
            <line p1="(xneg, 0.42)" p2="(xpos, 0.42)" stroke="black" thickness="1" arrows="2"/>
            <line p1="(xneg, 0)" p2="(xneg, 0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <line p1="(xpos, 0)" p2="(xpos, 0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5*(xneg+xpos), 0.42)" alignment="n" offset="(0,3)"><m>d</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="rdist">
            <line p1="(xpos, -0.42)" p2="(xP, -0.42)" stroke="black" thickness="1" arrows="2"/>
            <line p1="(xpos, 0)" p2="(xpos, -0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <line p1="(xP, 0)" p2="(xP, -0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5*(xpos+xP), -0.42)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-3)"><m>r</m></label>
          </group>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="axis"
              text="A schematic of an electric dipole on a horizontal axis. A negative charge minus q and a positive charge plus q are separated by a distance d. A point P lies on the axis a distance r to the right of the positive charge" >
            <annotation ref="negcharge" text="The negative charge, minus q, on the left"/>
            <annotation ref="poscharge" text="The positive charge, plus q, a distance d to the right of the negative charge"/>
            <annotation ref="ddist" text="The separation d between the two charges"/>
            <annotation ref="pointP" text="The point P on the axis, to the right of the positive charge"/>
            <annotation ref="rdist" text="The distance r from the positive charge to the point P"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>
        <p>
          Use the Taylor series to approximate the electric field <m>E</m> at point <m>P</m> 
          for <m>d \ll r</m>. Show that the leading term in the approximation is proportional to 
          <m>d/r^3</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>To approximate the electric field <m>E</m> at point <m>P</m> 
           for <m>d \ll r</m>, we can use the Taylor series expansion for 
           the function <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{(r+x)^2}</m> around <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>We have:
<md>
          f(x) = \frac{1}{(r+x)^2} = \frac{1}{r^2} \left( 1 + \frac{x}{r} \right)^{-2}
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Using the binomial series expansion, we can write:
<md>
          \left( 1 + \frac{x}{r} \right)^{-2} = 1 - 2\frac{x}{r} + 3\frac{x^2}{r^2} - 4\frac{x^3}{r^3} + \cdots
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Substituting <m>x=d</m> and <m>x=0</m>, we get:
<md>
          f(d) = \frac{1}{(r+d)^2} = \frac{1}{r^2} \left( 1 - 2\frac{d}{r} + 3\frac{d^2}{r^2} - 4\frac{d^3}{r^3} + \cdots \right)
        </md>
<md>
          f(0) = \frac{1}{r^2}
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Now, we can write the electric field <m>E</m> as:
<md>
          E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right) = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{1}{r^2} - \frac{1}{(r+d)^2} \right)
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Substituting the Taylor series expansion for <m>f(d)</m> and <m>f(0)</m>, we get:
<md>
          E = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{1}{r^2} - \frac{1}{r^2} \left( 1 - 2\frac{d}{r} + 3\frac{d^2}{r^2} - 4\frac{d^3}{r^3} + \cdots \right) \right)
        </md>
</p>
   
        <p>Simplifying, we have:
<md>
          E = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( 2\frac{d}{r^3} - 3\frac{d^2}{r^4} + 4\frac{d^3}{r^5} - \cdots \right)
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Thus, the leading term in the approximation is:
<md>
          E \approx \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \cdot 2\frac{d}{r^3}
        </md>
which shows that the leading term is proportional to <m>d/r^3</m>.</p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-dipole-field">
  <caption>The axial field of a dipole, <m>E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
  \left(\frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2}\right)</m>, compared with its
  leading Taylor term for <m>d \ll r</m>. The leading term
  <m>\frac{2qd}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r^3} \propto \frac{d}{r^3}</m> overshoots
  slightly at small <m>r</m> but converges to the exact field as <m>r</m>
  grows.</caption>
  <image width="65%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="dipole-field">
      <diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="8">
        <!-- Dipole axial field: E = k(q/r^2 - q/(r+d)^2). Natural units k=q=d=1.
             Leading Taylor term for d << r is 2qd/(4 pi eps0 r^3), proportional to d/r^3. -->
        <definition>Eexact(r) = 1/r^2 - 1/(r+1)^2</definition>
        <definition>Elead(r) = 2/r^3</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(1.15, -0.04, 6.4, 0.66)">
          <grid-axes xlabel="r" ylabel="E" hlabels="(2, 1, 6)" vlabels="(0.2, 0.2, 0.6)"/>
          <graph at="lead" function="Elead" domain="(1.5, 6)" stroke="red" thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
          <graph at="exact" function="Eexact" domain="(1.5, 6)" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
          <label at="leadlabel" anchor="(2.35, Elead(2.35))" alignment="ne" offset="(4,6)" color="red">
            <m>\dfrac{2qd}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r^3} \propto \dfrac{d}{r^3}</m>
          </label>
          <label at="exactlabel" anchor="(3.4, Eexact(3.4))" alignment="se" offset="(4,-6)" color="blue">
            <m>E = \dfrac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\!\left( \dfrac{q}{r^2} - \dfrac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)</m>
          </label>
          <label at="regime" anchor="(5.2, 0.32)" alignment="c">
            <m>d \ll r</m>
          </label>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="exact"
              text="The axial electric field of a dipole as a function of distance r. The exact field, E equals the constant times q over r squared minus q over quantity r plus d squared, is shown together with its leading Taylor term for d much less than r, which is 2 q d over 4 pi epsilon naught r cubed and is proportional to d over r cubed. The two curves separate at small r but converge as r grows, showing that the leading term captures the far-field behavior">
            <annotation ref="exact" text="The exact dipole field, decreasing with distance" sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="lead" text="The leading Taylor term, proportional to d over r cubed, which overshoots slightly but approaches the exact field for large r" sonify="yes"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>
    </solution>   
    
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-dipole-tolerance">
  <title>How small must the separation be?</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A dipole is often treated as a <term>point dipole</term> by replacing
      the exact axial field
      <md>
        E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left( \frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)
      </md>
      with its leading-order Taylor term for <m>d \ll r</m>,
      <md>
        E \approx \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\cdot\frac{2qd}{r^3}.
      </md>
      Suppose this approximation must agree with the exact field to within a
      relative error of <m>1\%</m>. If the field point <m>P</m> is
      <m>r = 3.0\ \text{cm}</m> from the positive charge, how small must the
      charge separation <m>d</m> be for the point-dipole approximation to be
      valid?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>
      The dominant error comes from the <em>next</em> term in the expansion.
      Using
      <m>E = \dfrac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left( \dfrac{2d}{r^3} -
      \dfrac{3d^2}{r^4} + \cdots \right)</m>, form the relative error of the
      leading term and keep only the largest contribution.
    </p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>d \lesssim \dfrac{2}{3}(0.01)\,r \approx 0.020\ \text{cm} = 0.20\ \text{mm}.</m>
    </p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      From the Taylor expansion,
      <md>
        E = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left( \frac{2d}{r^3} - \frac{3d^2}{r^4}
        + \cdots \right),
      </md>
      the leading term is <m>E_{\text{lead}} = \dfrac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\cdot
      \dfrac{2d}{r^3}</m>, and the first neglected term is
      <m>\dfrac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\cdot\dfrac{3d^2}{r^4}</m>. The relative
      error of the approximation is therefore
      <md>
        \frac{\left| E_{\text{lead}} - E \right|}{E}
          \approx \frac{\dfrac{3d^2}{r^4}}{\dfrac{2d}{r^3}}
          = \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{d}{r}.
      </md>
      Notice that <m>q</m> and <m>\epsilon_0</m> cancel, so the relative error
      depends only on the ratio <m>d/r</m>. Requiring this to be at most
      <m>1\% = 0.01</m> gives
      <md>
        \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{d}{r} \le 0.01
        \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
        d \le \frac{2}{3}(0.01)\,r.
      </md>
      With <m>r = 3.0\ \text{cm}</m>,
      <md>
        d \le \frac{2}{3}(0.01)(3.0\ \text{cm}) = 0.020\ \text{cm} = 0.20\ \text{mm}.
      </md>
      So the point-dipole approximation is accurate to <m>1\%</m> only when the
      charges are separated by less than about <m>0.2\ \text{mm}</m> at this
      distance<mdash/>consistent with <xref ref="fig-dipole-field"/>, where the
      two curves visibly merge as <m>r</m> grows relative to <m>d</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
</worksheet>
